1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
 Welcome to this short course on Lanchain for Large Language Model Application Development.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:09,000
 By prompting an LLM, or Large Language Model,

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
 it is now possible to develop AI applications much faster than ever before.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 But an application can require prompting an LLM multiple times and parsing its output,

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
 and so there's a lot of glue code that needs to be written.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
 Lanchain, created by Harrison Chase, makes this development process much easier.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
 I'm thrilled to have Harrison here, who had built this short course in collaboration with deeplearning.ai to teach how to use this amazing tool.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,000
 Thanks for having me. I'm really excited to be here.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 Lanchain started as an open source framework for building LLM applications.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 It came about when I was talking to a bunch of folks in the field who were building more complex applications

11
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
 and saw some common abstractions in terms of how they were being developed.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 And we've been really thrilled at the community adoption of Lanchain so far.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
 And so look forward to sharing it with everyone here and look forward to seeing what people build with it.

14
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,000
 And in fact, as a sign of Lanchain's momentum, not only does it have numerous users,

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 but there are also many hundreds of contributors to the open source.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
 And this has been instrumental for its rapid rate of development.

17
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 This team really ships code and features at an amazing pace.

18
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 So hopefully, after this short course, you'll be able to quickly put together some really cool applications using Lanchain.

19
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,000
 And who knows, maybe you even decide to contribute back to the open source Lanchain effort.

20
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 Lanchain is an open source development framework for building LLM applications.

21
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
 We have two different packages, a Python one and a JavaScript one.

22
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
 They're focused on composition and modularity.

23
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 So they have a lot of individual components that can be used in conjunction with each other or by themselves.

24
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
 And so that's one of the key value adds.

25
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
 And then the other key value add is a bunch of different use cases.

26
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 So chains of ways of combining these modular components into more end-to-end applications

27
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:08,000
 and making it very easy to get started with those use cases.

28
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 In this class, we'll cover the common components of Lanchain.

29
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 So we'll talk about models.

30
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 We'll talk about prompts, which are how you get models to do useful and interesting things.

31
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
 We'll talk about indexes, which are ways of ingesting data so that you can combine it with models.

32
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 And then we'll talk about chains, which are more end-to-end use cases, along with agents,

33
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
 which are a very exciting type of end-to-end use case, which uses the model as a reasoning engine.

34
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:40,000
 We're also grateful to Ankhush Gola, who is a co-founder of Lanchain alongside Harrison Chase,

35
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
 for also putting a lot of thoughts into these materials and helping with the creation of this short course.

36
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:54,000
 And on the deep learning.ai side, Jeff Lodwick, Eddie Hsu, and Diala Ezzedine have also contributed to these materials.

37
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:01,000
 And so with that, let's go on to the next video where we'll learn about Lanchain's models, prompts, and pauses.

